  "translation": {
    "literal": "Beneath a pine I ask the young boy; he says his master has gone to gather herbs. \"He is only somewhere in this mountain, the clouds are deep, and I do not know where.\"",
    "poetic": "Under a pine I question the boy. \"My master has gone to gather herbs,\" he says. \"He is on this mountain, that is all I know, but the clouds are thick, and where, I cannot say.\""
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "dialogue",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "A conversation with the question removed",
      "body": "Only the visitor's first act is described (問). Lines two to four are the boy's reply, given without quotation marks. The poem never says what the visitor asked, how he feels, or whether he goes away. The poem lets the clouds do the speaking: the hermit is close and out of reach, which is how a hermit should be."
    },
    {
      "id": "author",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "Who wrote it?",
      "body": "The poem is usually credited to Jia Dao (779–843), a monk-turned-poet known for polishing each line. Some old sources credit it to Sun Ge under the title 訪羊尊師, so the attribution is not entirely settled."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 去 · 處",
      "body": "Lines two and four end in 去 (qù) and 處 (chù), which rhyme in -u. Both were in the departing tone (去聲) in Tang speech. Here 處 is \"place\", chù, not chǔ (\"to deal with\")."
    },
    {
      "id": "readings",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Words with more than one reading",
      "body": "只 is read zhǐ (\"only\"). Some printings write 祇 for the same word. 處 is read chù in 知處 (\"know where\"). 藥 is yào."
    }
  ],
